B4: swap anonymous pages, and the two bugs it exposed

B3 could reclaim zeros, clean text and COW shares, but not the kind of
page a program actually fills memory with: its own dirty heap and stack.
Those had no backing store, so an exhausted pool meant killing somebody.
Swap is the missing half.

  * the disk image is now [MINIX fs 1MB][raw swap 2MB]: mkminix appends
    a zeroed region and the kernel addresses it directly by LBA
    (SWAP_START_LBA).  It is a raw device, not a file, which is why the
    reclaim path needs no buffers and no inode; 512 slots of 4KB;
  * the page-table entry remembers where the page went: present clear,
    bit 11 as the marker, slot number in the address bits (the CPU
    ignores everything but P when P is 0), so no per-page bookkeeping;
  * reclaim order is now zero page (memset) -> clean text (read back
    from the executable) -> dirty private page (write to swap) -> COW
    share (unmap only, as a last resort);
  * do_no_page recognises a swap entry and reads the page back instead
    of handing out a fresh zero page;
  * copy_page_tables brings a swapped page back before sharing it on
    fork, or the child would treat it as never-touched and get zeros;
  * free_page_tables returns the slot on exit - `slots free` returning
    to 512 in memstat is the regression for that.

Two real bugs on the way:

1. Swapping out sleeps (it writes to the disk) while the reclaimer is
   holding *another* task's page-table pointer.  That task can exit and
   have its address space freed during the write, so on return pt[i],
   t->pid and free_page(pa) are all stale - use-after-free, which showed
   up as a kernel page fault at 0xffffffff with a garbage pid.  Fixed by
   re-validating the task slot and pg_dir after the sleep and giving up
   (returning the slot) when they changed.  B3 never had this problem
   because none of its reclaim paths sleep.

2. With that fixed it still would not converge: once zeros and text ran
   out, the only victims were dirty private pages, which the owner
   touched again immediately, got swapped back in, and were chosen again
   - the machine spent all its time shuffling the same pages.  The fix
   is the textbook second-chance clock: the PTE Accessed bit (set by the
   hardware on every touch) is the reference bit, so a page used since
   the last sweep is skipped with its bit cleared, and only a second
   sweep that ignores the bit may evict it (which keeps it bounded).
   Swap-ins dropped from 49 to 28 and the scenario went from "does not
   converge" to 2/2 stable.

New scenario 24 'swap' on a 4MB machine: three children each fill 768KB
of heap and hold it (they time themselves out with alarm, so 142 is the
expected status and 139 would mean they were killed), while the parent
fills its own 768KB; every byte is verified afterwards.  The oom case is
re-tuned for the new threshold: exhaustion now needs more than memory
*and* swap (~1207 pages), so ten children ask for 1920.

Verified: make test 24/24 in 13m51s, make check green.
